WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:01.634
-Welcome, I guess, back

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.534
to "The Tonight Show,"

00:00:02.534 --> 00:00:04.234
even though you were on our show

00:00:04.234 --> 00:00:06.634
through Zoom or whatever.

00:00:06.634 --> 00:00:09.067
Yeah, but I appreciate
you being here in person.

00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:11.634
-Thank you for having me.
-Yeah.

00:00:11.634 --> 00:00:12.801
I have a lot
of questions for you.

00:00:12.801 --> 00:00:14.667
One, I'm just going
to start this off, is

00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:16.067
a few years back,

00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:18.767
Japanese Breakfast
released a song called

00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:20.100
"Jimmy Fallon Big!"

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.100
[ Laughter ]
-Yes.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.901
-Can you explain to me
what that was or...?

00:00:24.901 --> 00:00:27.734
[ Laughter ]
-Yes. Yeah.
So, for our second record --

00:00:27.734 --> 00:00:28.968
-Or maybe don't explain.

00:00:28.968 --> 00:00:30.667
♪♪

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:32.667
I like the rumor.
What is?

00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:34.901
-No, it's a good thing.
-Sure.

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:36.334
-Before Japanese Breakfast,
I used to be

00:00:36.334 --> 00:00:39.400
in a very unpopular punk band
called Little Big League

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:44.133
and our bass player left
to join another band,

00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:46.000
that was more popular.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.701
He sat me down at the table
and was like,

00:00:48.701 --> 00:00:50.634
"You know, I love this band.

00:00:50.634 --> 00:00:52.067
I wish we could continue
on together,

00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:53.667
but this other band
has invited me to play

00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:55.400
and we're going to be
Jimmy Fallon big

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.200
[ Laughter ]
and so I have
to pursue my dream."

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.934
And it's like, you know,
my bandmate is like a brother,

00:01:00.934 --> 00:01:03.901
so it felt like I was losing
a brother, in a way,

00:01:03.901 --> 00:01:07.000
and I felt all this shame
that we weren't big enough

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.968
to keep him in the band.

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:10.634
And then he --

00:01:10.634 --> 00:01:11.934
-He meant like
"The Tonight Show"?

00:01:11.934 --> 00:01:13.601
-Yes. Yeah.

00:01:13.601 --> 00:01:15.100
Actually, they never
even played here.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.567
They played "Seth Meyers," so.
-Okay.

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:19.968
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:01:19.968 --> 00:01:22.501
And then they fired him.
-Yeah.

00:01:22.501 --> 00:01:24.434
-And then a few years later,
I started Japanese Breakfast

00:01:24.434 --> 00:01:26.467
and we brought him back
into the band

00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:29.634
and then we actually got to play
"Jimmy Fallon Show" together.

00:01:29.634 --> 00:01:30.834
-That's a great story!

00:01:30.834 --> 00:01:33.133
[ Cheering and applause ]
Oh, I'm so happy!

00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:34.400
That's a great story.
-Yeah.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.734
-Oh, my gosh!
That's full circle.

00:01:37.734 --> 00:01:39.634
You've also been on the show
because you're one

00:01:39.634 --> 00:01:41.767
of the finalists
of our book club --

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:43.767
"Crying in H Mart."

00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:45.234
Man, congratulations on this.

00:01:45.234 --> 00:01:48.167
This was a genius piece of work.

00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:50.501
I loved it. It was beautiful.

00:01:50.501 --> 00:01:52.534
It was brilliant.

00:01:52.534 --> 00:01:55.501
We did a thing where I just read
the first line of every book.

00:01:55.501 --> 00:01:57.100
Can I just read it?
-That's intense. Yeah.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:58.634
-That's intense.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:58.634 --> 00:02:00.968
It really is.

00:02:00.968 --> 00:02:04.667
"Ever since my mom died,
I cry in H Mart."

00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:07.167
And that kind of sets up
the whole book for kind of --

00:02:07.167 --> 00:02:10.767
It was the perfect
first sentence of a book.

00:02:10.767 --> 00:02:12.167
Tell me about this
and how did you get

00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:15.734
to start writing the full thing?
Did it start as a book?

00:02:15.734 --> 00:02:18.200
-It started as an essay.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.300
I am half Korean
and my mom is Korean

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:25.334
and so, in a way,
it felt like I was losing

00:02:25.334 --> 00:02:28.067
a part of my culture,
on top of losing my mother,

00:02:28.067 --> 00:02:32.100
and so I started going to H Mart

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.000
and learning how to cook
Korean dishes.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.467
And so every time I came home,

00:02:35.467 --> 00:02:38.100
it was a way of me
remembering my mom

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.801
and staying connected
to my culture.

00:02:39.801 --> 00:02:43.501
And so, yeah, ever since
I started going to H Mart,

00:02:43.501 --> 00:02:45.901
I would get really emotional
seeing, you know,

00:02:45.901 --> 00:02:47.801
different things
that my mom used to make at home

00:02:47.801 --> 00:02:50.267
or seeing Korean parents
and their kids.

00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:54.000
And yeah, I just thought
it was kind of a cute story.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.667
-It's this beautiful story.
It is great

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:57.400
and now it's being turned
into a movie.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:58.567
-Yes.
-And you're writing it.

00:02:58.567 --> 00:02:59.701
-Yes, I'm writing it.
-This is fantastic.

00:02:59.701 --> 00:03:01.033
[ Cheering and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:03:01.033 --> 00:03:03.667
-What's it like writing?

00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:06.534
How's the screenplay going?

00:03:06.534 --> 00:03:09.100
-It's going well.
-Yeah? Good. Okay.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.000
I finished the first draft
and I'm working

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.033
on the revisions.
It's a really crazy process

00:03:13.033 --> 00:03:15.534
because so much
of writing a book is,

00:03:15.534 --> 00:03:17.267
you know, it's like
a really meandering process.

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:19.234
You get to kind of
take your time

00:03:19.234 --> 00:03:21.734
and, with a screenplay,
you have to really know

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:23.534
what all the acts are
and where it's going.

00:03:23.534 --> 00:03:27.167
And so, yeah, it's been really
different doing so much dialogue

00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:28.968
and it's kind of nice.
It's kind of feels like I am

00:03:28.968 --> 00:03:30.601
like having conversations
with my mom again

00:03:30.601 --> 00:03:32.801
because you get to literally
write her dialogue.

00:03:32.801 --> 00:03:34.434
-I imagine the soundtrack
is going to be

00:03:34.434 --> 00:03:36.801
fantastic as well.
-I hope so, yeah.

00:03:36.801 --> 00:03:40.067
I'm going to, hopefully,
write my Oscar bait original.

00:03:40.067 --> 00:03:42.934
[ Laughter ]
Join --

00:03:42.934 --> 00:03:44.234
-Yeah, exactly.
I know, exactly right.

00:03:44.234 --> 00:03:47.601
-Join Questlove
in the EGOT club, yeah.

00:03:47.601 --> 00:03:50.534
-I know you got nominated
for a Grammy Award,

00:03:50.534 --> 00:03:51.801
which, congratulations on that.
-Thank you.

00:03:51.801 --> 00:03:52.634
-That's a big deal.

00:03:52.634 --> 00:03:55.334
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:58.067
You said, going in,
you're like, "Okay,

00:03:58.067 --> 00:04:00.167
if I'm going to the Grammys,
I have one goal,"

00:04:00.167 --> 00:04:02.400
and what was that goal?

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.300
-Well, you know,
my goal was to win a Grammy,

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.033
but that didn't happen, so.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.033 --> 00:04:07.767
-Second goal, second goal.
-After after I had lost

00:04:07.767 --> 00:04:10.467
two Grammys, I was like,
"Well, I have to get a picture

00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:13.400
with BTS
[ Laughter ]

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.000
and send it to my aunt in Korea,

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.901
so she knows
that I have a real job."

00:04:16.901 --> 00:04:18.968
[ Laughter ]
And, yeah,

00:04:18.968 --> 00:04:20.667
so it was
during a commercial break.

00:04:20.667 --> 00:04:22.234
They had just performed.
It was amazing.

00:04:22.234 --> 00:04:23.868
And then Megan Thee Stallion
came out

00:04:23.868 --> 00:04:26.100
and took a selfie
and my drummer and producer,

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:27.434
who was sitting at the table
with me is like,

00:04:27.434 --> 00:04:29.200
"No, go, this is your chance."

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:31.300
And so I went over
and I was like --

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:33.467
[ Speaking Korean ]

00:04:33.467 --> 00:04:35.400
And I was like, "Oh, maybe they
won't be like annoyed,

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:36.601
if I like say it in Korean."

00:04:36.601 --> 00:04:38.267
And they were kind of like,
"Oh, okay."

00:04:38.267 --> 00:04:40.234
And I don't know if that's even
the right way to say it

00:04:40.234 --> 00:04:41.767
in Korean.
I think that might mean,

00:04:41.767 --> 00:04:45.868
"I want photo, please."

00:04:45.868 --> 00:04:47.601
[ Laughter ]
-I get it.

00:04:47.601 --> 00:04:49.868
-But I think they got it
and they were like okay with it.

00:04:49.868 --> 00:04:51.701
-Here's the photo right here.

00:04:51.701 --> 00:04:53.534
Look at you guys,
looking gorgeous. Come on.

00:04:53.534 --> 00:04:55.434
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:04:55.434 --> 00:04:56.334
Fantastic.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.667
But then you posted this
on the socials

00:05:03.667 --> 00:05:06.067
and it went ballistic.
-Yes. Yeah.

00:05:06.067 --> 00:05:07.801
I was like at the table
while like Billie Eilish

00:05:07.801 --> 00:05:08.934
was playing.
I was like, "Oh, my God,

00:05:08.934 --> 00:05:12.334
there's like 100,000
Army likes on this.

00:05:12.334 --> 00:05:14.334
-BTS Army.
There's nothing like those fans.

00:05:14.334 --> 00:05:15.834
They're the greatest.
Yeah.

00:05:15.834 --> 00:05:18.634
But I like this other photo
that you posted.

00:05:18.634 --> 00:05:22.634
It made me laugh and it just
made me love you even more.

00:05:22.634 --> 00:05:25.434
After the Grammys,
everyone usually goes to these

00:05:25.434 --> 00:05:28.100
crazy, big parties
or something wild and fun.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:32.033
You, instead, went to Nathan's
and had a hot dog and a beer.

00:05:32.033 --> 00:05:35.734
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:35.734 --> 00:05:39.601
Yes! Why? How?
How did this happen?

00:05:39.601 --> 00:05:42.234
-It was such a busy day, getting
ready, and I was so hungry.

00:05:42.234 --> 00:05:44.067
All I ate was like gruel
in the morning

00:05:44.067 --> 00:05:46.367
and so I like didn't want
to take off the outfit

00:05:46.367 --> 00:05:48.634
and get stuff on it,
so I just like

00:05:48.634 --> 00:05:50.334
didn't really eat anything
that day.

00:05:50.334 --> 00:05:52.534
And it was in the MGM Grand
this year

00:05:52.534 --> 00:05:55.033
and so, on our way to the show,

00:05:55.033 --> 00:05:57.534
we passed like a Nathan's
hot dog in the food court

00:05:57.534 --> 00:05:59.901
and I was just like, "Oh,
I'm going to hit that up later."

00:05:59.901 --> 00:06:02.100
[ Laughter ]
And, the entire show,

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:04.467
I was just kind of like,
"I can't wait to go there."

00:06:04.467 --> 00:06:07.467
[ Laughter ]
And we actually left like
during the last award,

00:06:07.467 --> 00:06:10.067
me and Craig Hendrix,
who is our live drummer

00:06:10.067 --> 00:06:13.567
and he's also the producer
of our Grammy-nominated record.

00:06:13.567 --> 00:06:15.167
We went to the food court
and got

00:06:15.167 --> 00:06:16.801
like a two-for-one hot dog deal.

00:06:16.801 --> 00:06:18.067
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:06:18.067 --> 00:06:20.133
And it was just really sweet
because we were just

00:06:20.133 --> 00:06:22.501
in a food court
in Las Vegas at the Grammys

00:06:22.501 --> 00:06:26.100
and it was like two DIY kids
who, you know, stayed up late

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:28.467
in a freezing cold warehouse,
making this record,

00:06:28.467 --> 00:06:30.534
eating a lot of junk food,
full circle,

00:06:30.534 --> 00:06:32.234
like at the Grammys,
getting to share a hot dog,

00:06:32.234 --> 00:06:33.534
so it was really sweet.
-Isn't it great,

00:06:33.534 --> 00:06:35.567
how life works sometimes?
-Yeah, it was really sweet.

00:06:35.567 --> 00:06:37.567
-I just thought maybe, in honor
of "Saturday Night Live,"

00:06:37.567 --> 00:06:39.033
maybe we could destroy
a couple dogs.

00:06:39.033 --> 00:06:40.868
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:06:40.868 --> 00:06:43.133
-Oh, yes, yes, yes!
-Cheers.

00:06:43.133 --> 00:06:45.300
-Yes. Cheers.
-Congrats on everything.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.367
-Thank you.
-Michelle Zauner.

00:06:47.367 --> 00:06:51.467
Japanese Breakfast,
"Crying in H Mart,"

00:06:51.467 --> 00:06:54.300
and she's on "Saturday Night
Live" tomorrow night!

